#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b74365-7af7-533c-a4b6-82b95ad8c598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295869} {:answer/id #uuid "4dac0845-ece5-5c93-ac78-e3107df5c303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295870} {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} {:answer/id #uuid "b49dba43-1fc2-53b2-b7f9-3b4142fab53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872} {:answer/id #uuid "3e965958-eaee-5339-9cf7-a5c18c0224a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978ca961-fc0f-5ab9-9bc4-26f6aa707375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79b0e9e-ce07-5367-ae03-a5fb5855bf88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "116a80ae-5b7a-5a13-919a-314322a246f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143035" :question/problem "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} {:answer/id #uuid "08fff3d5-f006-5e51-b487-252567288d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb91c8b-41a8-5201-a728-dda49efb372f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295885} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1903b-8dd1-5d3e-990b-7e0192bf2bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "ce285e3c-2f8f-5986-9c6a-2c74be616204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9392e9a-f656-5daf-b678-018e74b9f8bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91ea1276-29dd-5d34-8bf4-312e105d7100" :question/math-academy-id "q-143058" :question/problem "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$"}]}
